{
 "title": "Why Do We Have Seasons?",
 "lang": "en",
 "steps": [
  {
   "title": "The Big Myth vs. Reality",
   "say": "Many people think seasons happen because Earth gets closer or farther from the Sun. But Earth's orbit is nearly a circle!",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "main_title",
     "x": 800,
     "y": 50,
     "text": "Why Do We Have Seasons?",
     "size": 52,
     "color": "ink",
     "anchor": "middle",
     "underline": true
    },
    {
     "op": "rect",
     "id": "myth_box",
     "x": 120,
     "y": 140,
     "w": 640,
     "h": 130,
     "color": "red",
     "width": 3,
     "fill": "red"
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "myth_text",
     "x": 150,
     "y": 160,
     "text": "MYTH: Earth is closer to the Sun in summer.\nReality: In July, Earth is actually farthest!",
     "size": 30,
     "color": "red"
    },
    {
     "op": "rect",
     "id": "fact_box",
     "x": 840,
     "y": 140,
     "w": 640,
     "h": 130,
     "color": "green",
     "width": 3,
     "fill": "green"
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "fact_text",
     "x": 870,
     "y": 160,
     "text": "FACT: Earth's axis is tilted at 23.5°.\nThe tilt stays pointed in the same direction!",
     "size": 30,
     "color": "green"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Earth's Orbit and the Sun",
   "say": "Let's look at Earth revolving around the Sun throughout the year, keeping its axis tilted at 23.5 degrees.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "erase",
     "ids": [
      "myth_box",
      "myth_text",
      "fact_box",
      "fact_text"
     ]
    },
    {
     "op": "ellipse",
     "id": "orbit_path",
     "cx": 800,
     "cy": 450,
     "rx": 540,
     "ry": 160,
     "color": "gray",
     "width": 3,
     "dash": true
    },
    {
     "op": "ellipse",
     "id": "sun_body",
     "cx": 800,
     "cy": 450,
     "rx": 55,
     "ry": 55,
     "color": "red",
     "width": 4,
     "fill": "red"
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "sun_label",
     "x": 800,
     "y": 435,
     "text": "SUN",
     "size": 32,
     "color": "red",
     "anchor": "middle"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "June: Northern Summer",
   "say": "In June, the North Pole tilts toward the Sun. Sunlight hits the Northern Hemisphere directly, giving longer days and warmer weather.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "ellipse",
     "id": "earth_june",
     "cx": 260,
     "cy": 450,
     "rx": 42,
     "ry": 42,
     "color": "blue",
     "width": 4,
     "fill": "blue"
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "axis_june",
     "x1": 290,
     "y1": 380,
     "x2": 230,
     "y2": 520,
     "color": "ink",
     "width": 4
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "equator_june",
     "x1": 222,
     "y1": 435,
     "x2": 298,
     "y2": 465,
     "color": "gray",
     "width": 2,
     "dash": true
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "ray_june",
     "x1": 730,
     "y1": 450,
     "x2": 320,
     "y2": 450,
     "color": "red",
     "width": 3,
     "arrow": true
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "lbl_june",
     "x": 260,
     "y": 310,
     "text": "JUNE SOLSTICE",
     "size": 32,
     "color": "ink",
     "anchor": "middle"
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "desc_june",
     "x": 260,
     "y": 540,
     "text": "North tilts TOWARD Sun\n• North: Summer (warm & long days)\n• South: Winter (cold & short days)",
     "size": 26,
     "color": "ink",
     "anchor": "middle"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "December: Northern Winter",
   "say": "Six months later in December, the tilt hasn't changed direction, so now the South Pole leans toward the Sun instead.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "ellipse",
     "id": "earth_dec",
     "cx": 1340,
     "cy": 450,
     "rx": 42,
     "ry": 42,
     "color": "blue",
     "width": 4,
     "fill": "blue"
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "axis_dec",
     "x1": 1370,
     "y1": 380,
     "x2": 1310,
     "y2": 520,
     "color": "ink",
     "width": 4
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "equator_dec",
     "x1": 1302,
     "y1": 435,
     "x2": 1378,
     "y2": 465,
     "color": "gray",
     "width": 2,
     "dash": true
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "ray_dec",
     "x1": 870,
     "y1": 450,
     "x2": 1280,
     "y2": 450,
     "color": "red",
     "width": 3,
     "arrow": true
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "lbl_dec",
     "x": 1340,
     "y": 310,
     "text": "DECEMBER SOLSTICE",
     "size": 32,
     "color": "ink",
     "anchor": "middle"
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "desc_dec",
     "x": 1340,
     "y": 540,
     "text": "North tilts AWAY from Sun\n• North: Winter (cold & short days)\n• South: Summer (warm & long days)",
     "size": 26,
     "color": "ink",
     "anchor": "middle"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Why Angle Matters: Energy Concentration",
   "say": "Direct sunlight concentrates heat into a tight spot, while slanted sunlight spreads the same energy over a much larger area.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "erase",
     "ids": [
      "orbit_path",
      "sun_body",
      "sun_label",
      "earth_june",
      "axis_june",
      "equator_june",
      "ray_june",
      "lbl_june",
      "desc_june",
      "earth_dec",
      "axis_dec",
      "equator_dec",
      "ray_dec",
      "lbl_dec",
      "desc_dec"
     ]
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "angle_heading",
     "x": 800,
     "y": 140,
     "text": "The Mechanism: Direct vs. Slanted Sunlight",
     "size": 38,
     "color": "ink",
     "anchor": "middle"
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "ground_direct",
     "x1": 200,
     "y1": 460,
     "x2": 560,
     "y2": 460,
     "color": "ink",
     "width": 4
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "beam_dir_1",
     "x1": 340,
     "y1": 250,
     "x2": 340,
     "y2": 455,
     "color": "red",
     "width": 3,
     "arrow": true
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "beam_dir_2",
     "x1": 420,
     "y1": 250,
     "x2": 420,
     "y2": 455,
     "color": "red",
     "width": 3,
     "arrow": true
    },
    {
     "op": "rect",
     "id": "spot_direct",
     "x1": 340,
     "y": 462,
     "w": 80,
     "h": 14,
     "color": "red",
     "fill": "red"
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "txt_direct",
     "x": 380,
     "y": 500,
     "text": "Direct Sunlight (Summer)\n• High solar angle\n• Energy concentrated in small area\n• Maximum surface heating",
     "size": 28,
     "color": "ink",
     "anchor": "middle"
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "ground_slant",
     "x1": 1040,
     "y1": 460,
     "x2": 1400,
     "y2": 460,
     "color": "ink",
     "width": 4
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "beam_slant_1",
     "x1": 1100,
     "y1": 270,
     "x2": 1180,
     "y2": 455,
     "color": "blue",
     "width": 3,
     "arrow": true
    },
    {
     "op": "line",
     "id": "beam_slant_2",
     "x1": 1180,
     "y1": 270,
     "x2": 1320,
     "y2": 455,
     "color": "blue",
     "width": 3,
     "arrow": true
    },
    {
     "op": "rect",
     "id": "spot_slant",
     "x1": 1180,
     "y": 462,
     "w": 140,
     "h": 14,
     "color": "blue",
     "fill": "blue"
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "txt_slant",
     "x": 1220,
     "y": 500,
     "text": "Slanted Sunlight (Winter)\n• Shallow solar angle\n• Energy spread over large area\n• Weak surface heating",
     "size": 28,
     "color": "ink",
     "anchor": "middle"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Summary: The Two Key Factors",
   "say": "In summary, seasons are caused by Earth's 23.5-degree tilt and its revolution around the Sun.",
   "ops": [
    {
     "op": "rect",
     "id": "summary_card",
     "x": 250,
     "y": 660,
     "w": 1100,
     "h": 180,
     "color": "green",
     "width": 3,
     "fill": "green"
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "sum_title",
     "x": 800,
     "y": 680,
     "text": "KEY TAKEAWAY",
     "size": 34,
     "color": "green",
     "anchor": "middle"
    },
    {
     "op": "write",
     "id": "sum_content",
     "x": 800,
     "y": 730,
     "text": "1. Earth's 23.5° axial tilt + Orbit around Sun = Seasons\n2. Tilted towards Sun = Direct rays + Long days (Summer)\n3. Tilted away from Sun = Slanted rays + Short days (Winter)",
     "size": 28,
     "color": "ink",
     "anchor": "middle"
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
}